  "notes": "The MCA Nashville president-producer with 100+ No. 1 singles, modelled 1990–2016 because the research puts his 80s work out of window. The tracking rule is quoted and is the whole session block: takes are cut \"live, with the vocal cut live too — a documented departure from standard Nashville procedure where tracks come first and vocals later,\" under a stated rule for the room — \"here's the deal, guys, we all have to be as one before I'm gonna like it. Everybody be on your toes because the take is the take. No redo's.\" One-take ensemble capture with no comping is a humanize reading, not a mix one: the timing is human and consistent, the velocity spread is wide, and the fills sit where a band would put them rather than on an edit grid. Harmony is the second real delta and it is documented through his training: \"a classically trained pianist who played for Elvis, and the ii–V and secondary-dominant vocabulary in his productions reflects that,\" so ii–V–I leads the families and the seventh share runs well above the genre's triad-heavy default. The band template is listed instrument by instrument — \"bass, drums, fiddle/mandolin doubling, keys, pedal steel, two electric/acoustic guitarists, a dedicated flatpicking acoustic player and accordion\" — which is why the counter runs a genuine answer-lick density rather than a pad. The arrangement note is deliberately plain: \"sections are conventional but the performance is loose and dynamic.\" ⚠ The bass is stated as \"live bass only … no programmed low end,\" so no `drums.bass808` block. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the humanize values, the velocity tiers, the perc density and the counter ratio.",
